#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# wavelet_scalogram

#% text
# `wavelet_scalogram(d)` shows the magnitude of the wavelet coefficients against time and scale: for continuous wavelet data one row per scale, for discrete wavelet data of a list one row per leaf with every coefficient over the samples it covers. `wavelet_scalogram(d, sel)` uses the selected indices, `wavelet_scalogram(d, sel, f)` the values f(c) instead of |c|.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
wavelet_scalogram(discrete_wavelet_transform(table(sin(20*x) + sin(10*x), [x, 1, 10, 0.01]), automatic, 4))
#% output
#= image("wavelet_scalogram.files/wavelet_scalogram-1.svgz")

#% cell
wavelet_scalogram(continuous_wavelet_transform(table(sin(20*x) + sin(10*x), [x, 1, 10, 0.02])), [4 | 5 | 6 | 7, _])
#% output
#= image("wavelet_scalogram.files/wavelet_scalogram-2.svgz")

